Z||000003||Oreif||12-21-2000||11:44 AM||||I have an 86 SE with an automatic and I can "chirp" them from a light. A friends 87 GT can "light them up" and he has a 5-spd. Both vehicles have a stock 2.8L engine. (I have sprint manifolds but the rest is stock)<BR>Unless you are running some monster size tires, you should be able to at least squeal off the line. How many miles on the car? When you take off does the car seem to lose power at the higher RPM's? If you have a hard time accelerating when the tach gets to 3500-4000 RPM you may have a clogging catalytic converter.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Black88GT||12-21-2000||07:18 PM||||If you want a car to do burnouts or smoke shows, maybe you should check out an old Chevelle or Camaro.<P>The Fiero is mid-engined, RWD, which puts more weight over the drive wheels preventing them from spinning uncontrollably while you are racing.<P>Ask Trina about burnouts...<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000005||Xtreme Fiero's||12-21-2000||07:34 PM||||Two friends of mine hve '86 & 88's GT Auto's and neither one had any balls off the line at all. But they could both cruise 110-125 MPH just fine both toped out 120 (87) & 125 (88)||||reg||1
